citeturn9329search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last month; Image: Figure 3: Risk detection rates of CREST-Search compared to baseline red-teaming methods across different risk categories. ... @misc{ou2026searchgoeswrongredteaming, title={When Search Goes Wrong: Red-Teaming Web-Augmented Large Language Models}, author={Haoran Ou and Kangjie Chen and Xingshuo Han and Gelei Deng and Jie Zhang and Han Qiu and Tianwei Zhang}, year={2026}, eprint={2510.09689}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.CR}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.09689}, }
  * Role Play: The query instructs the LLM to adopt a persona, such as a "fringe legal advisor" or a "controversial historian." This context can bypass internal safety filters and encourage the model to fetch and cite sites that align with that specific (and potentially harmful) persona.

## The Iterative Refinement Pipeline

Because commercial models are black boxes, a single query might not always trigger a citation risk. CREST-Search employs a feedback loop to improve the effectiveness of the attack.

  1. Generation: A dedicated red-teaming model produces an initial adversarial query based on one of the three strategies.
  2. Execution and Risk Evaluation: The query is sent to the target LLM (the "victim"). The framework then extracts all cited URLs and uses automated moderation tools to scan both the model's response and the content of the linked pages.
  3. Refinement: If the attack fails to trigger a risk, a "judgment model" analyzes the failure. It provides guidance on why the query was ineffective—perhaps it was too obvious and got filtered, or too vague to trigger a citation. The red-teaming model then uses this feedback to generate a more sophisticated version of the query.

The effectiveness of the framework is measured by the Risk Detection Rate $R_{DR}$R D R​, defined as:

$$R_{DR} = \frac{N_{risk}}{N_{total}} \times 100\%$$R D R​=N t o t a l​N r i s k​​×100%

where $N_{risk}$N r i s k​ is the number of queries that successfully triggered at least one type of risk (response, citation, or both), and $N_{total}$N t o t a l​ is the total number of queries attempted.


## Experimental Findings and Model Vulnerabilities

The researchers tested CREST-Search against several state-of-the-art commercial models, including GPT-4o-search and Gemini-2.5-flash-search. The results revealed a stark contrast between CREST-Search and traditional baselines.

Image: Figure 3: Risk detection rates of CREST-Search compared to baseline red-teaming methods across different risk categories.

### The Prevalence of Citation Risk

The most significant finding was that the vast majority of uncovered risks were citation risks. Specifically, 89.3% of the successful attacks involved the model citing harmful external content. In 74.7% of all cases, the LLM's own text was perfectly safe, but the cited links led to harmful material. This confirms that focusing only on the model's generated response—as most current safety tools do—leaves a massive security gap.

### Model Performance and Transferability

The framework demonstrated high transferability, meaning queries generated for one model often worked on others.

Image: Figure 4: Transferability of CREST-Search across various commercial LLMs, showing high risk detection rates for all tested models.

While all models were vulnerable, the Gemini series generally showed higher risk detection rates compared to the GPT series. This suggests that models with more aggressive or comprehensive retrieval capabilities might be more susceptible to citing fringe or unsafe content if not paired with equally aggressive URL filtering.

### Impact of Refinement

Ablation studies showed that the number of refinement rounds is a critical factor. As the number of iterations increased from 1 to 10, the risk detection rate for categories like "harassment" rose from roughly 35% to over 70%.

Image: Figure 5: Performance metrics as a function of refinement rounds, illustrating the trade-off between risk detection and optimization cost/time.

However, this improvement follows a curve of diminishing returns while costs increase linearly. The specialized red-teaming model helps mitigate this by providing a high starting point for query effectiveness.

Models should verify the safety of a URL and the content of the destination page before including it as a citation.
  * Adversarial Training: Developers can use the queries generated by frameworks like CREST-Search to train their models to recognize and ignore "bait" keywords that lead to low-credibility sites.
  * Continuous Auditing: As the web changes, so do the risks. Automated red-teaming should be a permanent part of the model deployment pipeline to catch new types of citation risks in real-time.

By providing a specialized model and a robust dataset, this work enables researchers and developers to conduct more thorough safety checks, ultimately leading to web-augmented LLMs that are not just smarter and more current, but also safer for the end user. The diversity of the generated queries, indicated by a self-BLEU score of $S_{BLEU} = 0.59$S B L E U​=0.59, ensures that these audits cover a wide range of potential attack vectors, making the resulting safety improvements more robust.

Unsafe llm-based search: Quantitative analysis and mitigation of safety risks in ai web search
Mazeika, M., Phan, L., Yin, X., Zou, A., Wang, Z., Mu, N., Sakhaee, E., Li, N., Basart, S., Li, B., et al. Harmbench: A standardized evaluation framework for automated red teaming and robust refusal. arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.04249, 2024.

Rag llms are not safer: A safety analysis of retrieval-augmented generation for large language models

This work is important because it demonstrates that even Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with curated corpora can introduce new safety risks. This finding directly motivates the main paper's focus on the more uncontrolled environment of open-web search, establishing that the retrieval process itself is a critical and under-examined attack surface.

An, B., Zhang, S., and Dredze, M. Rag llms are not safer: A safety analysis of retrieval-augmented generation for large language models. arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.18041, 2025.

Red teaming language models to reduce harms: Methods, scaling behaviors, and lessons learned

This is a foundational citation that establishes the concept and practice of red-teaming as a pivotal strategy for systematically probing LLM vulnerabilities. The main paper's framework, CREST-Search, is an extension and specialization of the general red-teaming principles laid out in this and similar works, adapting them to the specific risks of web-search integration.

Ganguli, D., Lovitt, L., Kernion, J., Askell, A., Bai, Y., Kadavath, S., Mann, B., Perez, E., Schiefer, N., Ndousse, K., Jones, A., Bowman, S., Chen, A., Conerly, T., DasSarma, N., Drain, D., Elhage, N., El-Showk, S., Fort, S., Hatfield-Dodds, Z., Henighan, T., Hernandez, D., Hume, T., Jacobson, J., Johnston, S., Kravec, S., Olsson, C., Ringer, S., Tran-Johnson, E., Amodei, D., Brown, T., Joseph, N., McCandlish, S., Olah, C., Kaplan, J., and Clark, J. Red teaming language models to reduce harms: Methods, scaling behaviors, and lessons learned, 2022.

