citeturn9330search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; # Less Diverse, Less Safe: The Indirect But Pervasive Risk of Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models ... When candidate diversity is artificially curtailed, TTS pipelines become highly susceptible to producing unsafe outputs, bypassing advanced reasoning strategies and standard safety guardrails. ...     @inproceedings{nahin2026less,

# Less Diverse, Less Safe: The Indirect But Pervasive Risk of Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models

Shahriar Kabir Nahin, Hadi Askari, Muhao Chen, Anshuman Chhabra

ICML 2026 5

Test-Time Scaling (TTS) has emerged as a powerful technique to enhance the reasoning capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs). By generating multiple candidate responses and selecting the best one using reward models or search algorithms like Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) or Best-of-N, TTS aims to produce more reliable outputs. However, this approach relies on a tacit, unexamined premise: that the pool of candidate responses is sufficiently diverse.

Recent research reveals a critical safety flaw in this assumption. When candidate diversity is artificially curtailed, TTS pipelines become highly susceptible to producing unsafe outputs, bypassing advanced reasoning strategies and standard safety guardrails.

## The Vulnerability in Test-Time Scaling

The AI industry generally assumes that scaling compute at inference time yields safer and more accurate results. 
## Limitations and Future Directions

While highly effective, REFDIV is a genetic algorithm that requires multiple iterations, making it compute-intensive (though the authors note it is comparable to existing baselines like GCG). The study primarily focused on Best-of-N and MCTS, leaving other TTS methods like Tree-of-Thought untested, though the underlying vulnerability is expected to persist.

Crucially, the authors note that simply increasing the number of candidates ($N$) does not solve the problem; it merely adds computational overhead. The findings underscore an urgent need for "diversity-aware" TTS systems capable of detecting and mitigating intentional mode collapse during the generation process.

