# Less Diverse, Less Safe: The Indirect But Pervasive Risk of Test-Time Scaling in Large Language Models

Shahriar Kabir Nahin, Hadi Askari, Muhao Chen, Anshuman Chhabra

arXiv 2025

Read Paper Bookmark

## TL;DR

Test-Time Scaling (TTS) improves LLM performance by generating and selecting from multiple candidate responses, assuming a diverse pool ensures reliability. This paper uncovers a critical vulnerability: by subtly constraining candidate diversity, an adversary can induce mode collapse and easily steer TTS systems into generating harmful outputs. The authors introduce REFDIV, a novel stress-testing protocol that consistently bypasses state-of-the-art guardrails and transfers across both open- and closed-source models.

## What Problem Does This Paper Address?

Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly using Test-Time Scaling (TTS) techniques—such as Best-of-N sampling or Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)—to boost their reasoning capabilities at inference time. These methods rely on the implicit assumption that exploring a wide, diverse solution space allows the model to filter out bad or unsafe paths.
