Figure 1: Comparison of existing LLM alignment strategies. (1) Fine-tuning methods (e.g.,
SFT, RLHF) modify parameters but suffer from high compute costs, long training times, and poor
generalization. (2) Fine-tuning free methods (e.g., promp-driven, output filtering, activation steer-
ing) avoid retraining yet lack precision and effective steering capability. Energy-Driven Steering,
offers the combined advantages of deployment flexibility, precise discrimination, and effective steer-
ing, compared with fine-tuning and fine-tuning free methods.
