<visual_element id="e1">
Figure 1: Comparison of existing LLM alignment strategies. The figure contrasts “Fine-tuning” versus “Fine-tuning Free.” Under Fine-tuning are SFT, DPO, and RLHF with “Data I” and “Data II,” showing “Iterative Training” and “Resource-Intensive time-consuming.” Under Existing Methods are “Prompt Driven” (Precise), “Content Filtering” (Passive), and “Activation Steering” (Ineffective), with notes about “Weak discrimination” and “Limited steering control.” Under Energy-Driven Steering (Ours) are “1. Deployment flexibility,” “2. Precise discrimination,” and “3. Effective steering,” with “Input,” “Output,” “Inference Time,” “Trained EBM Model,” and “Gradient-Based Steering,” plus a legend indicating black square markers for “E (undesirable)” and green square markers for “E (desirable).”
</visual_element>

Figure 1: Comparison of existing LLM alignment strategies. (1) Fine-tuning methods (e.g., SFT, RLHF) modify parameters but suffer from high compute costs, long training times, and poor generalization. (2) Fine-tuning free methods (e.g., prompt-driven, output filtering, activation steering) avoid retraining yet lack precision and effective steering capability. Energy-Driven Steering, offers the combined advantages of deployment flexibility, precise discrimination, and effective steering, compared with fine-tuning and fine-tuning free methods.

To address these limitations, we introduce **Energy-Driven Steering (EDS)**, a novel, fine-tuning free framework that resolves the tension between safety and helpfulness through dynamic, inference-time intervention. Our core idea is to interpret the LLM’s internal state through the lens of an energy landscape. We deploy a lightweight, external EBM (LeCun et al., 2006) that learns to assign a scalar “energy” value to the LLM’s hidden activations. This EBM is trained via contrastive learning to create an energy landscape where trajectories leading to undesirable outputs (like false refusals) have high energy, while trajectories for desirable, helpful responses have low energy. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
without compromising safety. While the underlying technique of activation steering could be considered a dual-use technology, our research is purely methodological and focuses on its pro-social application. By transparently reporting our methods and results on public datasets, we contribute to the responsible development of more robustly aligned AI systems. (https://openreview.net/pdf/5bfa898af50faed8e2b66fbdb8a3eceeb6345501.pdf)
