Image: Crest app icon

862 ·GitHub·

Crest

What is this?ImageImageCrest is an open source browser for Mac, iPhone, and iPad, built with SwiftUI. It organizes browsing into separate 'Spaces,' each with its own cookies, history, credentials, and sync identity.

Does it use one rendering engine or two?ImageImageOn Mac, Crest uses two engines: Chromium (based on ungoogled-chromium, with Google integrations removed) by default, and WebKit for Reader, translation, content blocking and protected video. iPhone and iPad use WebKit only.

How do I get Crest running?ImageImageOn Mac, download the signed, notarized build from GitHub Releases or run 'brew install --cask crest-browser'. iPhone and iPad builds come from the App Store, with a beta on TestFlight.

Any catch before I install it?ImageImageCrest requires an Apple silicon Mac on macOS 26.1+ (or iOS/iPadOS 26.1+) — there's no Windows, Linux, or Intel Mac support, and Chrome Web Store extensions only run on Mac's Chromium pages.

Is Crest only for Apple users?ImageImageYes — Crest is Apple-only, shipping for Mac, iPhone, and iPad with no Android, Windows, or Linux builds.

What happens to my data in a Private Space?ImageImagePrivate Spaces use temporary website storage and stay out of normal history, restoration, and sync, and can require device authentication; passwords are kept in the system Keychain and excluded from iCloud CloudKit sync.

What exactly is a 'Space' in Crest?ImageImageA Space is an isolated browsing context with its own cookies, history, tabs, credentials, settings, and sync identity — switching Spaces changes the whole browsing environment, not just the window color.

Can I use my Chrome extensions in Crest?ImageImageYes, on Mac's Chromium pages you can install Chrome Web Store extensions per Space, though compatibility varies; extensions are not available on WebKit pages or on iPhone/iPad.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
