L209: # [NeurIPS 2026] FreqCa: Accelerating Image Generation and Editing via Frequency-Aware Caching
L210: 
L211: Jiacheng Liu^{1,2,3}, Peiliang Cai^{1}, Qinming Zhou^{1,4}, Yuqi Lin^{1,5}, Deyang Kong^{1,7}, Benhao Huang^{1,6}, Yupei Pan^{1,7}, Haowen Xu^{1}, Chang Zou^{1,2,7}, Junshu Tang^{2}, Shikang Zheng^{1,8}, Linfeng Zhang^{1}
L212: 
L213: ^{1}EPIC Lab, SJTU   ^{2}Tencent Hunyuan   ^{3}SDU   ^{4}THU   ^{5}JLU   ^{6}CMU   ^{7}UESTC   ^{8}SCUT
L214: The 【91†arXiv paper†arxiv.org】 is an earlier version. Please refer to the 【86†latest PDF】 for the NeurIPS 2026 version.
L215: 
L216: Qwen-Image samples generated with FreqCa at 7.14× FLOPs acceleration.
L217: ## 🔥 News
L218: 
L219:   * 2026/09/29 — The inference code for FLUX and Qwen-Image is released.
L220:   * 2026 — FreqCa is accepted at NeurIPS 2026. 🎉
L221: Abstract
L222: Diffusion transformers deliver strong image generation and editing quality, but their iterative inference is expensive. Existing feature-caching methods typically assume that adjacent diffusion features are uniformly similar or continuous. FreqCa shows that this assumption behaves differently across frequency bands: low-frequency components are highly similar but difficult to extrapolate, while high-frequency components are less similar yet more temporally continuous.
L223: Based on this observation, FreqCa directly reuses the low-frequency component and forecasts the high-frequency component with a second-order Hermite predictor. It additionally caches only the Cumulative Residual Feature (CRF), instead of layer-wise activations, reducing cache memory by approximately 99%. Experiments on FLUX.1-dev, FLUX.1-Kontext-dev, Qwen-Image, and Qwen-Image-Edit demonstrate strong acceleration for both image generation and editing without additional training.
L224: ## 💡 Method
L225: 
L226: Overview of the FreqCa framework.
L227: 
L228: FreqCa applies a different strategy to each frequency band of the Cumulative Residual Feature:
L229:   * Frequency-aware caching: reuse stable low-frequency structure and forecast evolving high-frequency details.
L230:   * Second-order Hermite predictor: preserves local trajectory curvature under aggressive cache intervals.
L231:   * CRF-only cache: changes cache-memory complexity from `O(L)` to `O(1)` with respect to transformer depth.
L232:   * Plug-and-play: requires no training or calibration data.
L233: ## 📊 Main Results
L234: 
L235: The 50-step FLUX experiments were measured on an NVIDIA A100, while the Qwen-Image experiments were measured on an NVIDIA H20.
L236: 
